= Generalization =
{{:Generalization/Simple}}
== Scope and distinctions ==
'''Generalization''' is extending a claim, pattern, skill, or inference beyond the particular observations or circumstances in which it was first established. Its warrant depends on what is carried across, the target circumstances, and evidence that relevant conditions remain applicable.

In [[Inductive reasoning]], generalization is ampliative: observed cases can support a broader conclusion without logically guaranteeing it. In psychology and education, transfer concerns applying prior learning in a different task or context. In empirical research, external validity asks whether conclusions apply to a specified population, setting, intervention, or time beyond the study conditions. These uses overlap but are not interchangeable.

[[Related to::Generalization (machine learning)|Generalization (machine learning)]] addresses a narrower computational problem: performance of fitted models on data beyond their training examples. This page does not replace its treatment of train/test partitions, overfitting, or distribution shift.

== Conditions and failure modes ==
A useful generalization specifies the source cases, target domain, relevant similarities and differences, and a way to test transfer. Sampling bias, confounding, omitted context, and changes in underlying mechanisms can invalidate the extension. A universal claim requires substantially stronger support than a bounded claim about a particular population.

